WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.338
Here's a snake on a ten by ten board. It's eight

2
00:00:03.438 --> 00:00:07.207
segments long, and there's a pellet over on the right.

3
00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:10.364
It can't turn back on itself, so it

4
00:00:10.464 --> 00:00:13.620
has three moves. Up, left, or right.

5
00:00:13.716 --> 00:00:18.786
Jev, TypeSafe's System One model, sees the board as plain text.

6
00:00:18.883 --> 00:00:21.730
It doesn't think out loud. In one pass,

7
00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:24.904
it hands back a probability for each move.

8
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.011
Ninety-five percent: go right, toward the pellet. And that

9
00:00:29.111 --> 00:00:32.981
one answer takes about a hundred and sixty milliseconds.

10
00:00:33.066 --> 00:00:37.900
Echo is a far smaller network. Twenty-two thousand parameters,

11
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.923
small enough to run on a laptop's CPU.

12
00:00:41.016 --> 00:00:44.850
It reads the same board, split into three grids: where the

13
00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:48.377
body is, where the head is, and where the pellet is.

14
00:00:48.466 --> 00:00:53.707
It learned by matching Jev's answers on about thirty-eight thousand boards. Not

15
00:00:53.807 --> 00:00:57.763
just Jev's favorite move, but how sure it was of every move.

16
00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:02.323
On this board it says right, ninety-two percent. And it answers

17
00:01:02.423 --> 00:01:06.824
in a fifth of a millisecond. About eight hundred times faster.

18
00:01:06.924 --> 00:01:09.801
Next to Jev's bar, you can barely see it.

19
00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:14.286
So they agree. Here's what right actually does.

20
00:01:14.383 --> 00:01:18.775
They're both confidently wrong. That's the snake's own body.

21
00:01:18.866 --> 00:01:22.501
Echo copied Jev, blind spots included. Being eight

22
00:01:22.601 --> 00:01:26.162
hundred times faster doesn't make it any smarter.

23
00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:29.421
But speed buys something else. In the time

24
00:01:29.521 --> 00:01:32.927
Jev gives one answer, Echo can give hundreds.

25
00:01:33.016 --> 00:01:36.152
So we can leverage that. Echo plus search tries

26
00:01:36.252 --> 00:01:39.387
each move, and looks at the board that follows.

27
00:01:40.283 --> 00:01:44.290
Then every move from each of those, and every move after

28
00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:48.250
that, seven moves deep. Echo scores a whole level in a

29
00:01:48.350 --> 00:01:51.991
single batch. Red is a future where the snake died.

30
00:01:52.083 --> 00:01:57.364
Three hundred sixty-three boards, seventy-seven milliseconds. Still

31
00:01:57.464 --> 00:01:59.935
less time than Jev's one answer.

32
00:02:00.033 --> 00:02:04.233
Now work backwards, from the bottom. A future where the snake

33
00:02:04.333 --> 00:02:08.111
survives is worth the pellets it ate along the way. One

34
00:02:08.211 --> 00:02:11.777
where it dies loses its whole score, plus a penalty.

35
00:02:11.866 --> 00:02:15.587
Each node blends its children's values, leaning on the

36
00:02:15.687 --> 00:02:19.267
moves Echo likes, unless those moves turn out badly.

37
00:02:19.366 --> 00:02:22.797
Up at the top. Right had ninety-two percent of

38
00:02:22.897 --> 00:02:26.713
Echo's vote. But its future is worth minus fifteen.

39
00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:29.823
Weight each move by P, times e to the

40
00:02:29.923 --> 00:02:33.960
v. Minus fifteen shrinks right to almost nothing.

41
00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:37.876
Up and left both survive, so their values tie, and the

42
00:02:37.976 --> 00:02:42.384
search just keeps Echo's preference between them. It plays up.

43
00:02:42.483 --> 00:02:45.975
That's the whole rule, at every node. Echo's

44
00:02:46.075 --> 00:02:49.485
intuition, reweighted by what the tree saw.

45
00:02:49.583 --> 00:02:54.018
So the snake goes up, around its own body, and gets the pellet.

46
00:02:57.216 --> 00:03:00.363
That danger was one step away. A simple rule

47
00:03:00.463 --> 00:03:03.683
would have caught it. Here's one it wouldn't.

48
00:03:03.783 --> 00:03:07.939
Later in the same game, the pellet is right below the head.

49
00:03:08.033 --> 00:03:11.037
Jev never made it this far. Its own game on

50
00:03:11.137 --> 00:03:15.079
this board crashed at move thirty-four. But we can still

51
00:03:15.179 --> 00:03:18.905
ask it about this position. Down, ninety-six percent.

52
00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:23.267
Echo agrees. Ninety-seven percent. Take it.

53
00:03:23.366 --> 00:03:25.929
Taking it is perfectly safe, for one

54
00:03:26.029 --> 00:03:28.741
move. Follow it down the tree, though.

55
00:03:28.833 --> 00:03:31.949
One line survives each level, then none. By

56
00:03:32.049 --> 00:03:35.315
the fifth move, every future that starts with

57
00:03:35.415 --> 00:03:39.130
down is dead. Going right, though, keeps branching.

58
00:03:39.216 --> 00:03:42.583
So all of the search's weight goes right.

59
00:03:43.016 --> 00:03:46.071
Here's why. The pellet sits at the mouth of a

60
00:03:46.171 --> 00:03:49.155
dead end, walled in by the snake's own body.

61
00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:54.176
Echo on its own takes the pellet, and crashes on the very next move.

62
00:03:54.266 --> 00:03:59.349
The search goes right first. In those two moves, the tail pulls out of the

63
00:03:59.449 --> 00:04:03.902
way. Then it takes the same pellet, and the dead end has an exit.

64
00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:09.382
Over twenty boards that no training ever touched, Jev averaged seven

65
00:04:09.482 --> 00:04:12.899
and a half pellets a game. Echo, about the same.

66
00:04:12.983 --> 00:04:17.191
Echo plus search averaged thirty-nine, beat Jev on all twenty

67
00:04:17.291 --> 00:04:20.792
boards, and still took less time per move than Jev.

68
00:04:20.883 --> 00:04:24.555
So Echo didn't get better judgment than Jev. It got cheap

69
00:04:24.655 --> 00:04:27.997
enough to look several moves ahead in the same time.

